var e={module:`html`,levels:[{title:`First Steps`,blurb:`Meet the skeleton of a page: tags, headings, paragraphs and lists.`},{title:`Links & Images`,blurb:`Connect pages and add pictures, with attributes that make them work.`},{title:`Semantic Pages`,blurb:`Give your page meaning with landmarks, sections and head tags.`},{title:`Forms & Tables`,blurb:`Collect input with friendly forms and show data in tables.`},{title:`Media & Access`,blurb:`Add video and audio, and make every page work for everyone.`}],questions:[{id:`html-001`,level:1,topic:`Basics`,type:`single`,prompt:`What does the browser show for this snippet?`,code:`<h1>Hello</h1>`,lang:`html`,choices:["The text `<h1>Hello</h1>` with the tags showing",`Nothing, because the tags hide the text`,`The word "Hello" as a big heading`,`The word "Hello" in small grey text`],answer:2,hint:`Browsers do not print tags. They use tags to decide how text looks.`,explain:'The browser reads the tags but does not show them. `<h1>` marks a top-level heading, so "Hello" appears as large, bold heading text.'},{id:`html-006`,level:1,topic:`Tags`,type:`single`,prompt:"In this snippet, what is `</p>` called?",code:`<p>Hello</p>`,lang:`html`,choices:[`A closing tag`,`A comment`,`An opening tag`,`An attribute`],answer:0,hint:"Look at the slash right after the `<`.",explain:"A tag with a slash, like `</p>`, is a closing tag. It marks where the element ends. The opening tag `<p>` marks where it starts."},{id:`html-016`,level:1,topic:`Tags`,type:`tf`,prompt:"HTML tag names are case-insensitive, so `<P>` and `<p>` mean the same thing.",answer:!0,hint:`Try to remember if browsers care about uppercase in tag names.`,explain:"Browsers read tag names case-insensitively. Lowercase is the common convention, so most code uses `<p>`."},{id:`html-004`,level:1,topic:`Headings`,type:`single`,prompt:`Which heading element is the most important (top level)?`,choices:["`<h3>`","`<h6>`","`<head>`","`<h1>`"],answer:3,hint:`Headings go from 1 to 6. Which end is the top?`,explain:"`<h1>` is the top-level heading, usually the main title of the page. `<h2>` to `<h6>` are smaller sub-levels."},{id:`html-003`,level:1,topic:`Structure`,type:`single`,prompt:`Which tag fills both blanks?`,code:`<html>
  <head>
    <title>My page</title>
  </head>
  <____>
    <h1>Hello!</h1>
  </____>
</html>`,lang:`html`,choices:["`meta`","`title`","`main`","`body`"],answer:3,hint:"This element is the sibling of `<head>` inside `<html>`.",explain:"A page has two parts inside `<html>`: `<head>` for information about the page and `<body>` for everything shown on screen."},{id:`html-002`,level:1,topic:`Doctype`,type:`single`,prompt:"What does `<!DOCTYPE html>` at the very top of a page do?",choices:[`It links the page to the browser's default stylesheet`,`It sets the text that shows in the browser tab`,`It loads the browser's built-in library of HTML tags`,`It tells the browser this is a modern HTML5 page`],answer:3,hint:`It is a short declaration about how the browser should read the page.`,explain:`The doctype tells the browser this is a modern HTML5 page, so it follows today's rules (called "standards mode"). Without it, browsers fall back to an old "quirks mode" that copies historic bugs.`},{id:`html-021`,level:1,topic:`Text`,type:`multi`,prompt:`Which of these are real HTML elements? Select all that apply.`,choices:["`<b>`","`<i>`","`<bold>`","`<italic>`"],answer:[0,1],hint:`Real HTML tag names are often very short.`,explain:"`<b>` and `<i>` are real elements, shown in bold and italics by default. There are no `<bold>` or `<italic>` elements, so browsers treat them as unknown tags."},{id:`html-022`,level:1,topic:`Lists`,type:`fill`,prompt:"Type the tag name of the element for one item in a list (for example `p`).",answer:[`li`,`<li>`],hint:"This element goes inside both `<ul>` and `<ol>`.",explain:'`<li>` stands for "list item". Every item in a bulleted or numbered list is an `<li>`.'},{id:`html-015`,level:1,topic:`Emphasis`,type:`single`,prompt:`Which element marks text as strongly important (bold by default)?`,choices:["`<strong>`","`<mark>`","`<em>`","`<important>`"],answer:0,hint:"This element is about importance. `<em>` is about stress, which is a different job.",explain:"`<strong>` means the text has strong importance, and browsers show it in bold. There is no `<important>` element in HTML."},{id:`html-008`,level:1,topic:`Lists`,type:`single`,prompt:`Which tag fills both blanks to make an unordered (bulleted) list?`,code:`<____>
  <li>Milk</li>
  <li>Eggs</li>
</____>`,lang:`html`,choices:["`ul`","`li`","`ol`","`dl`"],answer:0,hint:`Bullets show that the order of the items does not matter.`,explain:"`<ul>` is an unordered list, shown with bullets by default. Each item inside it is an `<li>`."},{id:`html-009`,level:1,topic:`Lists`,type:`single`,prompt:`How does the browser show these items by default?`,code:`<ol>
  <li>Crack the eggs</li>
  <li>Whisk them</li>
  <li>Cook gently</li>
</ol>`,lang:`html`,choices:[`On one line, separated by commas`,`With numbers 1, 2, 3`,`With no markers at all`,`With bullet points`],answer:1,hint:'The "o" in `<ol>` means "ordered".',explain:"`<ol>` is an ordered list, so the browser numbers the items. Use it when the order matters, like steps in a recipe."},{id:`html-018`,level:1,topic:`Structure`,type:`tf`,prompt:"In an HTML page, the `<html>` element wraps both `<head>` and `<body>`.",code:`<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
  <head>...</head>
  <body>...</body>
</html>`,lang:`html`,answer:!0,hint:`Picture the outermost box of the page.`,explain:"`<html>` is the root element. It contains exactly two child elements: `<head>` and `<body>`."},{id:`html-010`,level:1,topic:`Head`,type:`single`,prompt:"Where does the `<title>` element belong?",choices:["Inside `<h1>`","Before `<html>`","Inside `<body>`","Inside `<head>`"],answer:3,hint:`The title is information about the page, not visible page content.`,explain:"`<title>` goes inside `<head>`, together with other information about the page. Every page needs one."},{id:`html-011`,level:1,topic:`Head`,type:`single`,prompt:"Where do users usually see the text of the `<title>` element?",choices:[`As a large heading at the top of the page`,`In the browser tab`,`As a tooltip over every link`,`At the bottom of the page`],answer:1,hint:`Look at the top of your browser window.`,explain:"The `<title>` text appears in the browser tab, in bookmarks, and in search results. It is not shown inside the page itself."},{id:`html-020`,level:1,topic:`Comments`,type:`multi`,prompt:`Which of these are valid HTML comments? Select all that apply.`,choices:["`<!-- Main menu -->`","`<!--Main menu-->`","`// Main menu`","`/* Main menu */`"],answer:[0,1],hint:`Two of these styles come from JavaScript and CSS.`,explain:"An HTML comment starts with `<!--` and ends with `-->`, with or without spaces inside. `//` and `/* */` are comments in JavaScript and CSS, so in HTML they show up as plain text."},{id:`html-023`,level:1,topic:`Files`,type:`fill`,prompt:"Type the usual file name for a website's home page, including the extension (for example `about.html`).",answer:[`index.html`,`index.htm`],hint:"Servers look for this file first when a URL ends with `/`.",explain:"When a URL points to a folder, like `example.com/`, most servers send the `index.html` file in it. So the home page of a simple site is usually `index.html`."},{id:`html-005`,level:1,topic:`Paragraphs`,type:`single`,prompt:`Which tag creates a paragraph of text?`,choices:["`<pg>`","`<text>`","`<para>`","`<p>`"],answer:3,hint:`HTML often uses very short names for common elements.`,explain:"The `<p>` element marks a paragraph. Browsers add some space above and below it by default."},{id:`html-013`,level:1,topic:`Line Breaks`,type:`single`,prompt:`Which element adds a line break inside a paragraph?`,choices:["`<br>`","`<lb>`","`<hr>`","`<break>`"],answer:0,hint:`It is a void element, so it has no closing tag.`,explain:"`<br>` starts a new line inside the same block of text, which is handy for addresses or poems. `<hr>` is a thematic break between sections."},{id:`html-017`,level:1,topic:`Head`,type:`tf`,prompt:"Content inside `<head>` is shown in the main area of the page.",answer:!1,hint:`Think about where the visible content of a page goes.`,explain:"The `<head>` holds information about the page, like its title and character set. Visible content goes in `<body>`."},{id:`html-012`,level:1,topic:`Nesting`,type:`single`,prompt:`Which snippet is nested correctly?`,choices:["`<strong><p>Hi</strong></p>`","`<p><strong>Hi</p></strong>`","`<p><strong>Hi<p></strong>`","`<p><strong>Hi</strong></p>`"],answer:3,hint:`The element you open last should close first.`,explain:"Elements must close in reverse order of opening, like boxes inside boxes. `<strong>` opens inside `<p>`, so it must close before `</p>`."},{id:`html-014`,level:1,topic:`Whitespace`,type:`single`,prompt:`How does the browser display this paragraph?`,code:`<p>Hello      world</p>`,lang:`html`,choices:[`"Hello      world" with all the spaces`,`"Hello" and "world" on two lines`,`Nothing, because of an error`,`"Hello world" with a single space`],answer:3,hint:`Browsers treat a run of spaces in HTML text in a special way.`,explain:"In normal text, the browser collapses several spaces (and line breaks) into one space. Use CSS or `<pre>` if you need to keep them."},{id:`html-007`,level:1,topic:`Comments`,type:`single`,prompt:`What does the browser show for this snippet?`,code:`<p>Hi</p>
<!-- <p>Bye</p> -->`,lang:`html`,choices:[`Only "Bye"`,`Only "Hi"`,`"Hi" followed by the comment text`,`Both "Hi" and "Bye"`],answer:1,hint:"Text between `<!--` and `-->` is a note for developers.",explain:"Everything between `<!--` and `-->` is a comment. The browser does not display it, even when it contains tags."},{id:`html-019`,level:1,topic:`Headings`,type:`multi`,prompt:`Which of these are real heading elements? Select all that apply.`,choices:["`<h2>`","`<h6>`","`<heading>`","`<h7>`"],answer:[0,1],hint:`Headings are numbered, but the numbers stop somewhere.`,explain:"HTML has six heading levels, `<h1>` to `<h6>`. There is no `<heading>` or `<h7>` element."},{id:`html-024`,level:1,topic:`Lists`,type:`fill`,prompt:"Type the tag name of the element that creates a numbered (ordered) list (for example `p`).",answer:[`ol`,`<ol>`],hint:"It is the partner of `<ul>`, and it also holds `<li>` items.",explain:"`<ol>` creates an ordered list, and the browser numbers its items for you."},{id:`html-025`,level:2,topic:`Images`,type:`single`,prompt:`Which element embeds an image in a page?`,choices:["`<image>`","`<photo>`","`<img>`","`<pic>`"],answer:2,hint:`It is a short, three-letter name.`,explain:"The `<img>` element shows a picture from an image file. There is no `<image>`, `<photo>` or `<pic>` element in HTML."},{id:`html-026`,level:2,topic:`Images`,type:`single`,prompt:"Which attribute name fills the blank so `<img>` knows which file to load?",code:`<img ___="dog.jpg" alt="A happy dog">`,lang:`html`,choices:["`file`","`link`","`src`","`url`"],answer:2,hint:`The image needs to know where its file comes from.`,explain:"The `src` (source) attribute holds the URL of the image file."},{id:`html-027`,level:2,topic:`Alt Text`,type:`single`,prompt:"What is the `alt` attribute on an image for?",choices:[`A tooltip that appears whenever the mouse is over the image`,`A text version of the image for people who cannot see it`,`A caption that the browser prints below the image`,`The file name the browser uses when saving the image`],answer:1,hint:`Think about a screen reader user, or a slow connection where the image fails.`,explain:"`alt` text describes the image for screen reader users, and browsers show it if the image fails to load. It is not a tooltip: the `title` attribute does that."},{id:`html-041`,level:2,topic:`Alt Text`,type:`tf`,prompt:"Browsers display an image's `alt` text on the page even when the image loads correctly.",answer:!1,hint:"When is `alt` text actually visible?",explain:"The `alt` text is not displayed when the image loads. It is read by screen readers and shown only if the image fails."},{id:`html-034`,level:2,topic:`Alt Text`,type:`single`,prompt:"An image is only decoration, like a swirl. What is the best `alt`?",choices:['`alt="image"`','`alt=""`',"Leave out the `alt` attribute",'`alt="decorative swirl image"`'],answer:1,hint:`You want screen readers to skip it, but still mark it as intentional.`,explain:'An empty `alt=""` tells assistive tech to skip the image. Leaving `alt` out is different: some screen readers then read the file name.'},{id:`html-039`,level:2,topic:`Images`,type:`single`,prompt:"Why add `width` and `height` attributes to an `<img>`?",choices:[`So the browser can reserve space before the image loads`,`So the image file downloads with a smaller file size`,`So the browser crops the image file to those exact sizes`,`So search engines know how big the image is`],answer:0,hint:`Think about content jumping around while a page loads.`,explain:"With `width` and `height`, the browser knows the image shape early and reserves space. This prevents the layout from jumping when the image arrives."},{id:`html-045`,level:2,topic:`Paths`,type:`multi`,prompt:`Which of these are relative URLs? Select all that apply.`,choices:["`https://example.com/about`","`../img/logo.png`","`http://example.org/logo.png`","`about.html`"],answer:[1,3],hint:"A relative URL has no scheme like `https:` at the start.",explain:"Relative URLs are resolved from the current page, like `about.html` or `../img/logo.png`. URLs that start with a scheme like `https:` are absolute."},{id:`html-048`,level:2,topic:`Links`,type:`fill`,prompt:"Type the attribute name that holds the destination URL of an `<a>` link.",answer:[`href`],hint:'You see it in every link, like `<a ___="/about">`.',explain:"`href` holds the URL that the link points to. Without it, an `<a>` is not a real link."},{id:`html-033`,level:2,topic:`Links`,type:`single`,prompt:`What happens when a user clicks this link?`,code:`<a href="#contact">Contact</a>`,lang:`html`,choices:['The page scrolls to the element with `id="contact"`',`The browser opens the contact list on the user's device`,`The browser reloads the page from the top`,"The browser downloads a file named `contact`"],answer:0,hint:"The `#` refers to something on the same page.",explain:"A link to `#contact` jumps to the element whose `id` is `contact` on the same page. These are often called fragment links."},{id:`html-028`,level:2,topic:`Links`,type:`single`,prompt:'What does `target="_blank"` do on this link?',code:`<a href="https://example.com" target="_blank">Visit</a>`,lang:`html`,choices:[`It opens the link in a new tab or window`,`It hides the link text until the user hovers over it`,`It downloads the linked page as a file`,`It shows an empty page instead of the linked page`],answer:0,hint:`"Blank" hints at a fresh, empty browsing context.`,explain:'`target="_blank"` opens the link in a new tab or window. The "blank" part means a fresh, empty tab, which then loads the link.'},{id:`html-042`,level:2,topic:`Attributes`,type:`tf`,prompt:'In `<img src="logo.png">`, `src` is the attribute name and `logo.png` is its value.',answer:!0,hint:"Look at which side of the `=` each part is on.",explain:"An attribute has a name and a value. Here the name is `src`, and the value `logo.png` is the file to load."},{id:`html-032`,level:2,topic:`Links`,type:`single`,prompt:`Which value fills the blank so the link opens the user's email app with a new message?`,code:`<a href="____">Email us</a>`,lang:`html`,choices:["`mail://hi@example.com`","`mailto:hi@example.com`","`email:hi@example.com`","`send:hi@example.com`"],answer:1,hint:"There is no `//` after this scheme.",explain:"The `mailto:` scheme opens the default email app and fills in the address. Similarly, `tel:` starts a phone call."},{id:`html-031`,level:2,topic:`Attributes`,type:`single`,prompt:"How many attributes does this `<a>` element have?",code:`<a href="/about" title="About us">About</a>`,lang:`html`,choices:[`3`,`2`,`4`,`1`],answer:1,hint:'Count the `name="value"` pairs inside the opening tag.',explain:'The attributes are `href` and `title`. The text "About" is the element\'s content, not an attribute.'},{id:`html-038`,level:2,topic:`Link Text`,type:`single`,prompt:`Which link text is most helpful for screen reader users?`,choices:[`Link to a page`,`Read the pricing guide`,`Click here to see more info`,`More`],answer:1,hint:`Screen reader users often list all links on a page, out of context.`,explain:`Link text should make sense on its own. "Read the pricing guide" says where the link goes, while "Click here to see more info" does not.`},{id:`html-044`,level:2,topic:`Inline Elements`,type:`multi`,prompt:`Which elements are inline by default (they flow inside a line of text)? Select all that apply.`,choices:["`<a>`","`<p>`","`<ul>`","`<strong>`"],answer:[0,3],hint:`Which ones can sit in the middle of a sentence?`,explain:"Links and `<strong>` sit inside a line of text. Paragraphs and lists are block elements that start on a new line."},{id:`html-047`,level:2,topic:`Alt Text`,type:`fill`,prompt:`Type the attribute name that gives an image its text alternative.`,answer:[`alt`],hint:`Screen readers read this attribute out loud instead of showing the image.`,explain:"The `alt` attribute holds the text alternative for an image. Every `<img>` should have one, even if it is empty for decoration."},{id:`html-037`,level:2,topic:`Emphasis`,type:`single`,prompt:`Which element adds stress emphasis to a word, usually shown in italics?`,choices:["`<strong>`","`<mark>`","`<small>`","`<em>`"],answer:3,hint:`It changes how you would say the word out loud.`,explain:"`<em>` marks stress emphasis, which changes how a sentence is read aloud. Browsers show it in italics by default."},{id:`html-030`,level:2,topic:`Text`,type:`single`,prompt:`Which element marks a short piece of computer code inside a sentence?`,choices:["`<code>`","`<script>`","`<program>`","`<pre>`"],answer:0,hint:`You need an inline element for text inside a sentence, so a block element is wrong.`,explain:"`<code>` marks a short piece of code, and browsers show it in a monospace font. `<script>` runs JavaScript, and `<pre>` keeps the spaces and line breaks of a whole block."},{id:`html-040`,level:2,topic:`Links`,type:`tf`,prompt:"An `<a>` element can wrap an `<img>`, so the whole image works as a link.",answer:!0,hint:`Think about clickable logos.`,explain:"Links can wrap images and other content, like a clickable logo. The image `alt` should then describe where the link goes."},{id:`html-036`,level:2,topic:`Attributes`,type:`single`,prompt:`Which tag is written with correct attribute syntax?`,choices:['`<img src="a.png", alt="Logo">`','`<img (src="a.png") alt="Logo">`','`<img src="a.png" alt="Logo">`','`<img src:"a.png" alt:"Logo">`'],answer:2,hint:'Attributes use `name="value"` and are separated by spaces.',explain:'Attributes are written as `name="value"` and separated by spaces. Commas, colons and brackets are not part of the syntax.'},{id:`html-035`,level:2,topic:`Lists`,type:`single`,prompt:'You want "Fruit" to have a sub-list. Where should the inner `<ul>` go?',code:`<ul>
  <li>Fruit</li>
  <li>Bread</li>
</ul>`,lang:`html`,choices:['Inside the "Fruit" `<li>`, after the text',"After the closing `</ul>` of the outer list","Directly inside the outer `<ul>`, between the `<li>` items","Inside a `<p>` before the list"],answer:0,hint:"A `<ul>` may only have `<li>` children.",explain:"A nested list goes inside an `<li>`. A `<ul>` may only contain `<li>` elements directly, so the sub-list belongs to the item it describes."},{id:`html-029`,level:2,topic:`Paths`,type:`single`,prompt:`Where does the browser look for this image file?`,code:`<img src="images/cat.jpg" alt="A sleeping cat">`,lang:`html`,choices:["In an `images` folder next to the current page","In an `images` folder at the root of the computer's disk",`In the browser's own folder for downloaded images`,`On a default image server that the browser knows`],answer:0,hint:`The path has no slash or domain at the start.`,explain:"A path like `images/cat.jpg` is relative. The browser resolves it from the location of the current page."},{id:`html-043`,level:2,topic:`Void Elements`,type:`multi`,prompt:`Which of these are void elements (they never have a closing tag)? Select all that apply.`,choices:["`<p>`","`<li>`","`<img>`","`<br>`"],answer:[2,3],hint:`Which ones can never hold text inside them?`,explain:"`<img>` and `<br>` hold no content, so they never have a closing tag. `<p>` and `<li>` wrap content and need `</p>` and `</li>`."},{id:`html-046`,level:2,topic:`Paths`,type:`fill`,prompt:"This page is in the `blog` folder. The file `logo.png` is in the folder above `blog`. Type the two characters that fill the blank.",code:`<!-- This page is blog/post.html -->
<img src="__/logo.png" alt="Logo">`,lang:`html`,answer:[`..`,`../`],hint:"It is the same shortcut you use with `cd` in a terminal to go up.",explain:"`..` means the parent folder, so `../logo.png` goes up one level from `blog/` and finds the file there. A single `.` means the current folder."},{id:`html-049`,level:3,topic:`Semantics`,type:`single`,prompt:`Which element should hold the dominant content that is unique to this page?`,choices:["`<section>`","`<main>`","`<body>`","`<header>`"],answer:1,hint:`It is the content that is not repeated across pages, like headers and menus.`,explain:"`<main>` wraps the central content of the page. Screen reader users can jump straight to it, past the header and navigation."},{id:`html-050`,level:3,topic:`Semantics`,type:`single`,prompt:`Which element is meant for a block of major navigation links?`,choices:["`<menu-bar>`","`<nav>`","`<aside>`","`<links>`"],answer:1,hint:`Its name is a short form of a longer word about moving around a site.`,explain:"`<nav>` marks a group of major navigation links, like the site menu. Assistive tech exposes it as a navigation landmark."},{id:`html-065`,level:3,topic:`Containers`,type:`tf`,prompt:"A plain `<div>` is announced by screen readers as a landmark region.",answer:!1,hint:"Does `<div>` carry any meaning by itself?",explain:"`<div>` has no meaning of its own, so it is not a landmark. Use semantic elements when you want regions that users can jump to."},{id:`html-061`,level:3,topic:`Semantics`,type:`single`,prompt:`Which element groups a thematic part of a page, usually with its own heading?`,choices:["`<chapter>`","`<nav>`","`<section>`","`<strong>`"],answer:2,hint:`Think of the parts of a long document, each with its own heading.`,explain:"`<section>` groups related content on one theme, usually starting with a heading. There is no `<chapter>` element, and `<div>` is the better choice when you only need a styling wrapper."},{id:`html-054`,level:3,topic:`Semantics`,type:`single`,prompt:`Which element marks a thematic break between paragraphs, often shown as a line?`,choices:["`<hr>`","`<line>`","`<break>`","`<br>`"],answer:0,hint:`It is a void element with a two-letter name.`,explain:"`<hr>` marks a thematic break, like a change of topic. `<br>` only breaks a line inside the same text."},{id:`html-053`,level:3,topic:`Quotes`,type:`single`,prompt:`Which element is for a longer quotation from another source?`,choices:["`<blockquote>`","`<quote>`","`<cite>`","`<pre>`"],answer:0,hint:`Think about where a long quote sits: in its own block, not inside a sentence.`,explain:"`<blockquote>` holds a longer, block-level quotation. `<cite>` is for the title of a work, and there is no `<quote>` element."},{id:`html-068`,level:3,topic:`Block Elements`,type:`multi`,prompt:`Which elements are block-level by default (they start on a new line)? Select all that apply.`,choices:["`<p>`","`<a>`","`<div>`","`<span>`"],answer:[0,2],hint:`Which ones stack on top of each other like boxes?`,explain:"`<div>` and `<p>` are block elements, so each starts on a new line and takes the full width. `<span>` and `<a>` flow inline."},{id:`html-072`,level:3,topic:`Semantics`,type:`fill`,prompt:"Type the tag name of the element that usually holds copyright and contact links at the bottom of a page (for example `p`).",answer:[`footer`,`<footer>`],hint:`It is the opposite of the element that holds the logo at the top.`,explain:"`<footer>` holds closing content for a page or section, like copyright, contact links and related links."},{id:`html-057`,level:3,topic:`Language`,type:`single`,prompt:'What is the main job of `lang="en"` here?',code:`<html lang="en">`,lang:`html`,choices:[`It translates the page into English`,`It picks the programming language for scripts`,`It sets the keyboard layout for forms`,`It declares the language of the page content`],answer:3,hint:`Screen readers and translation tools want to know this.`,explain:"The `lang` attribute declares the language of the content. Screen readers use it to pick the right pronunciation, and browsers use it for translation offers."},{id:`html-062`,level:3,topic:`Head`,type:`single`,prompt:`Where does this line usually go?`,code:`<link rel="stylesheet" href="styles.css">`,lang:`html`,choices:["Inside `<head>`","At the very end of `<body>`","Inside `<main>`","Before `<!DOCTYPE html>`"],answer:0,hint:`It is information about the page, not visible content.`,explain:'A `<link rel="stylesheet">` goes in `<head>`, so the browser loads the CSS before showing the page.'},{id:`html-066`,level:3,topic:`Semantics`,type:`tf`,prompt:"The `<header>` element and the `<head>` element do the same job.",answer:!1,hint:`One is visible on the page, one is not.`,explain:"`<head>` holds information about the document, like `<title>`. `<header>` is visible introductory content, like a logo and menu."},{id:`html-063`,level:3,topic:`Meta`,type:`single`,prompt:`What is this tag mainly used for?`,code:`<meta name="description" content="Easy recipes for busy mornings.">`,lang:`html`,choices:[`Text that shows as a heading at the top of the page`,`A short summary that search engines may show in results`,`A hidden note for other developers who read the code`,`The text that appears in the browser tab for this page`],answer:1,hint:`Think about the grey text under a link on a search results page.`,explain:`The description meta tag gives a short summary of the page. Search engines often show it under the page title in results.`},{id:`html-056`,level:3,topic:`Head`,type:`single`,prompt:"Why do almost all pages put this line in the `<head>`?",code:`<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">`,lang:`html`,choices:[`So phones use their real width instead of zooming out`,`So the page loads faster on slow mobile networks`,`So search engines know the page is about mobile apps`,`So the browser hides the address bar while scrolling`],answer:0,hint:`Think about what happens when a page designed for a laptop opens on a small screen.`,explain:`Without it, phones pretend to be about 980px wide and shrink the whole page down. With it, the page uses the phone's real width, so your responsive CSS works. It does not change loading speed.`},{id:`html-058`,level:3,topic:`Headings`,type:`single`,prompt:`What would best fix the heading structure here?`,code:`<h1>Recipes</h1>
<h4>Breakfast</h4>
<p>Start the day well.</p>`,lang:`html`,choices:["Change `<h1>` to `<h4>`","Wrap both headings in `<p>`","Change `<h4>` to `<h2>`","Remove the `<h1>`"],answer:2,hint:`Heading levels should not skip steps going down.`,explain:'Headings form an outline, and screen reader users navigate by them. "Breakfast" is a direct sub-topic of "Recipes", so it should be an `<h2>`.'},{id:`html-067`,level:3,topic:`Landmarks`,type:`multi`,prompt:`Which elements create landmark regions that screen reader users can jump to? Select all that apply.`,choices:["`<b>`","`<span>`","`<main>`","`<nav>`"],answer:[2,3],hint:`Landmarks are the big regions of a page.`,explain:"`<main>` and `<nav>` map to landmark roles, so users can jump between them. `<span>` and `<b>` are inline and have no landmark role."},{id:`html-071`,level:3,topic:`Meta`,type:`fill`,prompt:`Type the missing attribute name that sets the character encoding.`,code:`<meta ______="utf-8">`,lang:`html`,answer:[`charset`],hint:`It is one lowercase word that describes how the text is encoded.`,explain:'`<meta charset="utf-8">` sets the character encoding. UTF-8 supports almost every language and emoji.'},{id:`html-051`,level:3,topic:`Containers`,type:`single`,prompt:`Which element is a generic inline container with no special meaning?`,choices:["`<p>`","`<section>`","`<div>`","`<span>`"],answer:3,hint:"You want the inline cousin of `<div>`.",explain:"`<span>` is a generic inline container, handy for styling part of a sentence. `<div>` is its block-level counterpart."},{id:`html-059`,level:3,topic:`Semantics`,type:`single`,prompt:"Why use semantic elements like `<nav>` and `<main>` instead of only `<div>`?",choices:[`They are required for CSS selectors to work on the page`,`They tell browsers and assistive tech what each part is`,`They add default colors and a layout to the page`,`They make the page download and render much faster`],answer:1,hint:`Think about who reads the HTML besides your eyes.`,explain:"Semantic elements say what content is, not how it looks. Assistive tech and search engines use that meaning, while a `<div>` tells them nothing."},{id:`html-064`,level:3,topic:`Semantics`,type:`tf`,prompt:"A page should have no more than one visible `<main>` element.",answer:!0,hint:`How many "main parts" can one page have?`,explain:"`<main>` marks the single main content area of the page. So a page should not show more than one at a time."},{id:`html-052`,level:3,topic:`Semantics`,type:`single`,prompt:`A blog post could be shared on its own somewhere else. Which element fits it best?`,choices:["`<section>`","`<div>`","`<article>`","`<aside>`"],answer:2,hint:`Ask: would it still make sense in a news feed or on another site?`,explain:"`<article>` is for self-contained content that makes sense on its own, like a post, a news story or a comment. A `<section>` is a part of a bigger whole, so it fits less well."},{id:`html-055`,level:3,topic:`Dates`,type:`single`,prompt:`Which element marks a date in a way machines can read?`,code:`<p>Published on <____ datetime="2026-10-29">29 October</____>.</p>`,lang:`html`,choices:["`<time>`","`<when>`","`<date>`","`<calendar>`"],answer:0,hint:"The `datetime` attribute is a clue.",explain:"`<time>` shows a human-friendly date and gives a machine-readable version in `datetime`. Search engines and tools can use it."},{id:`html-060`,level:3,topic:`Figures`,type:`single`,prompt:"Which element adds a caption to a `<figure>`?",choices:["`<legend>`","`<imgcaption>`","`<figcaption>`","`<caption>`"],answer:2,hint:"`<caption>` exists, but it belongs to tables.",explain:"`<figcaption>` goes inside `<figure>` to caption an image, chart or code sample. `<caption>` is the equivalent for tables, and there is no `<imgcaption>` element."},{id:`html-069`,level:3,topic:`Asides`,type:`multi`,prompt:"Which are good uses of `<aside>`? Select all that apply.",choices:[`A box that defines a term used in the article`,`The main article of the page`,`The whole page's main navigation`,`A sidebar with related links`],answer:[0,3],hint:"`<aside>` content is related, but not central.",explain:"`<aside>` holds content that is related to the main content but separate from it, like sidebars and side notes. Main content goes in `<main>`, and the site menu in `<nav>`."},{id:`html-070`,level:3,topic:`Semantics`,type:`fill`,prompt:"Type the tag name of the element for introductory content at the top of a page, like the logo and site name (for example `p`).",answer:[`header`,`<header>`],hint:`Think of the word for the top part of a letter or a document.`,explain:"`<header>` holds introductory content, like a logo, the site name and the main menu. Do not mix it up with `<head>`, which holds information about the document and is not shown."},{id:`html-073`,level:4,topic:`Forms`,type:`single`,prompt:`Which element wraps form controls and sends their data when submitted?`,choices:["`<submit>`","`<input>`","`<fieldset>`","`<form>`"],answer:3,hint:"The `action` and `method` attributes go on this element.",explain:"The `<form>` element wraps controls and submits their values. Its `action` and `method` attributes say where and how."},{id:`html-076`,level:4,topic:`Inputs`,type:`single`,prompt:"Which input `type` lets a user pick only one option from a group?",choices:["`toggle`","`checkbox`","`option`","`radio`"],answer:3,hint:`Checkboxes let users pick many options. You need the other common type.`,explain:`Radio buttons in the same group allow only one choice. Checkboxes allow any number of choices.`},{id:`html-075`,level:4,topic:`Inputs`,type:`single`,prompt:"Which `type` hides the characters as the user types them?",choices:["`hidden`","`secret`","`invisible`","`password`"],answer:3,hint:`Think of a login form.`,explain:'`type="password"` masks the typed characters. `type="hidden"` is different: it sends a value but shows no field at all, and `invisible` is not a real type.'},{id:`html-085`,level:4,topic:`Tables`,type:`single`,prompt:`Which element creates a header cell in a table?`,choices:["`<td>`","`<tr>`","`<thead>`","`<th>`"],answer:3,hint:`Its name is two letters long.`,explain:"`<th>` is a header cell, which labels a row or column. `<thead>` is a group that holds the header rows."},{id:`html-089`,level:4,topic:`Tables`,type:`tf`,prompt:`Tables are the recommended way to lay out the columns of a whole page.`,answer:!1,hint:`What kind of content are tables meant for?`,explain:`Tables are for tabular data, like a timetable. Use CSS (for example Flexbox or Grid) for page layout.`},{id:`html-078`,level:4,topic:`Labels`,type:`single`,prompt:"Why is `placeholder` not a good replacement for a `<label>`?",choices:[`It makes the field required, so users must type in it`,`It only works on search fields, not other inputs`,`It disappears as soon as the user starts typing`,`It sends its own text to the server with the form`],answer:2,hint:`What happens to the placeholder text after you start typing?`,explain:"Placeholder text vanishes once the user types, so they can forget what the field is for. A visible `<label>` stays and is reliably announced."},{id:`html-093`,level:4,topic:`Tables`,type:`multi`,prompt:`Which of these are real table elements? Select all that apply.`,choices:["`<td>`","`<tcell>`","`<trow>`","`<thead>`"],answer:[0,3],hint:`Real table tags are very short.`,explain:"`<thead>` groups header rows, and `<td>` is a data cell. Rows use `<tr>`, and there are no `<tcell>` or `<trow>` elements."},{id:`html-095`,level:4,topic:`Inputs`,type:`fill`,prompt:"Type the tag name of the element for multi-line text input, like a comment box (for example `p`).",answer:[`textarea`,`<textarea>`],hint:"Unlike `<input>`, this element has a closing tag, and its text goes between the tags.",explain:"`<textarea>` lets users type several lines. Unlike `<input>`, it has a closing tag, and its default text goes between the tags."},{id:`html-080`,level:4,topic:`Inputs`,type:`single`,prompt:`Which tag fills both blanks to create a drop-down list?`,code:`<____ name="color">
  <option>Red</option>
  <option>Blue</option>
</____>`,lang:`html`,choices:["`choice`","`select`","`dropdown`","`list`"],answer:1,hint:`It describes what the user does with the options.`,explain:"`<select>` creates a drop-down, and each choice is an `<option>`. It saves space when there are many options."},{id:`html-074`,level:4,topic:`Labels`,type:`single`,prompt:`What connects this label to the input?`,code:`<label for="email">Email</label>
<input id="email" type="email">`,lang:`html`,choices:[`The label is written before the input`,"The input's `type` is `email`",`Both elements have the word "email" in their text`,"The label's `for` value matches the input's `id`"],answer:3,hint:`Look for two attributes that share the same value.`,explain:'A `<label for="x">` belongs to the element with `id="x"`. Then clicking the label focuses the input, and screen readers announce the label.'},{id:`html-088`,level:4,topic:`Labels`,type:`tf`,prompt:"Clicking a properly connected `<label>` focuses its input, or toggles it if it is a checkbox.",answer:!0,hint:`Try clicking the text next to a checkbox on a good form.`,explain:`A connected label makes the clickable area bigger. That helps everyone, especially people on touch screens.`},{id:`html-084`,level:4,topic:`Forms`,type:`single`,prompt:"In this form, what does `action` set?",code:`<form action="/signup" method="post">
  <input name="email" type="email">
  <button>Sign up</button>
</form>`,lang:`html`,choices:[`The URL that receives the form data`,`How the data is encrypted before it is sent`,`The JavaScript function that runs on submit`,`The text shown on the submit button`],answer:0,hint:`It answers the question "where does the data go?"`,explain:"`action` is the URL that receives the data. `method` says how to send it, for example `post`."},{id:`html-083`,level:4,topic:`Forms`,type:`single`,prompt:`Which attribute sets the key under which an input's value is sent with the form?`,choices:["`id`","`class`","`for`","`name`"],answer:3,hint:"The server sees pairs like `email=sam@example.com`.",explain:"The `name` attribute is the key in the submitted data. An input without a `name` is not sent at all."},{id:`html-079`,level:4,topic:`Forms`,type:`single`,prompt:"Which element groups related controls, like a set of radio buttons, with a `<legend>` caption?",choices:["`<group>`","`<section>`","`<formgroup>`","`<fieldset>`"],answer:3,hint:`Think of a box drawn around a set of fields.`,explain:"`<fieldset>` groups related controls, and its `<legend>` describes the group. Screen readers announce the legend when users move into the group."},{id:`html-092`,level:4,topic:`Forms`,type:`multi`,prompt:`Which elements are form controls that hold a value the user can change? Select all that apply.`,choices:["`<input>`","`<label>`","`<legend>`","`<textarea>`"],answer:[0,3],hint:`Which ones can you type into?`,explain:"`<input>` and `<textarea>` hold user values. `<label>` and `<legend>` are captions that describe controls."},{id:`html-094`,level:4,topic:`Validation`,type:`fill`,prompt:`Type the attribute name that makes a form field mandatory.`,answer:[`required`],hint:`It is a boolean attribute, and the browser blocks submission while the field is empty.`,explain:"The `required` attribute is a boolean attribute: its presence alone turns it on. The browser checks it on submit."},{id:`html-086`,level:4,topic:`Tables`,type:`single`,prompt:"In this table, what does each `<tr>` create?",code:`<table>
  <tr><th>Name</th><th>Age</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Mia</td><td>7</td></tr>
</table>`,lang:`html`,choices:[`A row`,`A column`,`A single cell`,`The table caption`],answer:0,hint:"Count the `<tr>` elements, then look at how the table is laid out.",explain:"`<tr>` is a table row, and the cells inside it (`<th>` or `<td>`) form the columns."},{id:`html-081`,level:4,topic:`Buttons`,type:`single`,prompt:`What happens when the user clicks this button?`,code:`<form action="/save">
  <input name="title">
  <button>Save</button>
</form>`,lang:`html`,choices:["Nothing, because it has no `type`",`It reloads the page without sending data`,`It clears every field`,`It submits the form`],answer:3,hint:"A `<button>` inside a form has a default `type`.",explain:'Inside a form, a `<button>` defaults to `type="submit"`. Add `type="button"` when a button should not submit.'},{id:`html-090`,level:4,topic:`Labels`,type:`tf`,prompt:"A `<label>` can wrap its input directly instead of using `for` and `id`.",code:`<label>
  Name
  <input name="name">
</label>`,lang:`html`,answer:!0,hint:`Think about putting the input inside the label element.`,explain:"Wrapping works too. The label is connected to the input inside it, so you do not need `for` and `id`."},{id:`html-077`,level:4,topic:`Inputs`,type:`single`,prompt:`What makes these radio buttons work as one group?`,code:`<input type="radio" name="size" value="s"> Small
<input type="radio" name="size" value="m"> Medium
<input type="radio" name="size" value="l"> Large`,lang:`html`,choices:['They all use `type="radio"`',"They all share the same `name`","They all have a `value`",`They are next to each other in the code`],answer:1,hint:`Which attribute has the same value on all three?`,explain:"Radio buttons with the same `name` form a group, so picking one unselects the others. The `name` is also the key sent with the form."},{id:`html-082`,level:4,topic:`Validation`,type:`single`,prompt:`The user leaves this field empty and submits. What happens?`,code:`<form>
  <input type="email" name="email" required>
  <button>Join</button>
</form>`,lang:`html`,choices:[`The browser stops the submission and shows a message`,`The form is sent to the server with an empty value`,`The browser fills the field with the user's saved email`,`The page shows an error in the JavaScript console`],answer:0,hint:`Look at the attribute with no value.`,explain:"The `required` attribute turns on built-in validation. The browser blocks submission and asks the user to fill in the field."},{id:`html-087`,level:4,topic:`Inputs`,type:`single`,prompt:'What do you get by using `type="email"` instead of `type="text"`?',choices:[`The typed value is hidden, like in a password field`,`The field becomes required, so it cannot be left empty`,"Format checks and an `@` keyboard on phones",`The browser sends a test email to check the address`],answer:2,hint:`Think about validation and mobile keyboards.`,explain:'`type="email"` checks that the value looks like an email address when the form submits. Phones also show a keyboard with an `@` key. It does not make the field required.'},{id:`html-091`,level:4,topic:`Inputs`,type:`multi`,prompt:"Which are valid `type` values for `<input>`? Select all that apply.",choices:["`email`","`number`","`dropdown`","`paragraph`"],answer:[0,1],hint:`For long text and drop-downs there are other elements.`,explain:"`email` and `number` are real input types. Long text uses `<textarea>`, and drop-downs use `<select>`."},{id:`html-096`,level:4,topic:`Inputs`,type:`fill`,prompt:'Type the `type` value for an input that the user can tick on or off, like "I agree to the terms".',answer:[`checkbox`,`type="checkbox"`],hint:`Use it for a yes/no choice that works on its own, unlike radio buttons.`,explain:'`type="checkbox"` creates a box that the user can tick or untick. Each checkbox works on its own, unlike radio buttons in a group.'},{id:`html-097`,level:5,topic:`Video`,type:`single`,prompt:"What does the `poster` attribute on `<video>` set?",choices:[`The title shown in the browser tab while it plays`,`A backup video file to play if the first one fails`,`A subtitle file that shows text over the video`,`An image shown before the video starts playing`],answer:3,hint:`Think of a movie poster outside a cinema.`,explain:"`poster` sets a preview image that shows until the user plays the video. The video file itself comes from `src` or `<source>`."},{id:`html-098`,level:5,topic:`Video`,type:`single`,prompt:"Why does this video offer two `<source>` elements?",code:`<video controls>
  <source src="clip.webm" type="video/webm">
  <source src="clip.mp4" type="video/mp4">
</video>`,lang:`html`,choices:[`The browser plays the first format it supports`,`The second file is only used on phones`,`The browser plays both videos one after the other`,`The browser shows both videos side by side`],answer:0,hint:`Different browsers have supported different video formats.`,explain:"The browser checks each `<source>` in order and uses the first one it can play. The `type` attribute helps it skip formats early."},{id:`html-112`,level:5,topic:`Video`,type:`tf`,prompt:"Browsers often block videos that autoplay with sound, but they usually allow autoplay when the video is `muted`.",answer:!0,hint:`Think about how annoying a surprise sound can be.`,explain:"Most browsers block autoplay with sound to protect users from surprise noise. Adding `muted` (and `playsinline` for phones) usually lets `autoplay` work."},{id:`html-099`,level:5,topic:`Video`,type:`single`,prompt:`Which tag fills the blank to add English captions to this video?`,code:`<video src="talk.mp4" controls>
  <____ kind="captions" src="talk.vtt" srclang="en" label="English">
</video>`,lang:`html`,choices:["`caption`","`figcaption`","`subtitle`","`track`"],answer:3,hint:`It points to a timed text file, often in WebVTT format.`,explain:"`<track>` goes inside `<video>` and links a WebVTT file. Captions help deaf users and anyone watching without sound."},{id:`html-103`,level:5,topic:`Images`,type:`single`,prompt:'What does `loading="lazy"` do on an `<img>`?',choices:[`It makes the image fade in slowly once it has loaded`,`It waits until the image is close to the visible area`,`It loads the image only after all scripts have finished`,`It loads a lower-quality version of the image first`],answer:1,hint:`Think about what "lazy" means for work you can do later.`,explain:`Lazy loading waits until the user scrolls near the image. This saves data and makes the first view load faster.`},{id:`html-104`,level:5,topic:`Images`,type:`single`,prompt:"What is the main purpose of `srcset` here?",code:`<img
  src="photo-800.jpg"
  srcset="photo-400.jpg 400w, photo-800.jpg 800w, photo-1600.jpg 1600w"
  alt="A red bike by a lake">`,lang:`html`,choices:[`It loads the three images in a random order`,`It shows all three images side by side on wide screens`,`It uses the next file in the list if one fails to load`,`It lets the browser pick the best size for the screen`],answer:3,hint:"Look at the numbers with `w` after each file name.",explain:"`srcset` lists candidate files with their widths. The browser picks one that fits the screen, so small devices download smaller files."},{id:`html-116`,level:5,topic:`Audio`,type:`multi`,prompt:"Which attributes are valid on `<audio>`? Select all that apply.",choices:["`loop`","`muted`","`poster`","`alt`"],answer:[0,1],hint:`Audio has no picture to show.`,explain:"`loop` and `muted` work on `<audio>` (and `<video>`). `alt` is for images, and `poster` is only for `<video>`."},{id:`html-119`,level:5,topic:`Images`,type:`fill`,prompt:"Type the tag name of the element that wraps several `<source>` elements and one `<img>` to offer different image formats (for example `p`).",answer:[`picture`,`<picture>`],hint:"It is newer than `<img>` and lets the browser choose a modern format like WebP.",explain:"`<picture>` lets the browser choose the first `<source>` it supports, such as AVIF or WebP. The inner `<img>` is the fallback and carries the `alt` text."},{id:`html-101`,level:5,topic:`Buttons`,type:`single`,prompt:"Why use a `<button>` instead of a `<div>` with a click handler?",choices:[`It works with the keyboard and screen readers by default`,"It runs click handlers faster than a `<div>` can",`It is the only element that CSS hover styles work on`,`It looks the same in every browser and cannot be restyled`],answer:0,hint:`Try using a page with only the keyboard.`,explain:"A `<button>` is focusable, fires on Enter and Space, and tells screen readers it is a button. A `<div>` needs extra work to do all of that."},{id:`html-114`,level:5,topic:`Attributes`,type:`tf`,prompt:"Each `id` value should be unique within a page.",answer:!0,hint:"Think about how `<label for>` and `#links` find their target.",explain:"An `id` identifies one element. Labels, fragment links and scripts rely on it, so duplicates cause bugs."},{id:`html-105`,level:5,topic:`Embeds`,type:`single`,prompt:`Which element embeds another web page, like a map or a video player?`,choices:["`<frame>`","`<embed-page>`","`<include>`","`<iframe>`"],answer:3,hint:`It is like a window to another page, placed inside yours.`,explain:"`<iframe>` places another document inside your page. Give it a `title` so screen reader users know what it contains."},{id:`html-102`,level:5,topic:`ARIA`,type:`single`,prompt:"This button shows only a symbol. What does `aria-label` add?",code:`<button aria-label="Close">&times;</button>`,lang:`html`,choices:[`Text that appears on the button next to the symbol`,`A tooltip that appears when the mouse hovers over it`,`A keyboard shortcut that presses the button`,`An accessible name that screen readers announce`],answer:3,hint:`What would a screen reader say for "x" without it?`,explain:'`aria-label` gives the button an accessible name, so screen readers say "Close, button". It does not change what sighted users see.'},{id:`html-107`,level:5,topic:`Alt Text`,type:`single`,prompt:`What is the problem with this logo link?`,code:`<a href="/">
  <img src="logo.svg" alt="">
</a>`,lang:`html`,choices:[`Images are not allowed inside links, so the link breaks`,"The `href` must be a full URL like `https://example.com/`","SVG files cannot be shown with an `<img>` element","The empty `alt` leaves the link with no accessible name"],answer:3,hint:`What would a screen reader say for this link?`,explain:'The image is the only content of the link, so empty `alt` leaves the link with no name. Use something like `alt="Home"`.'},{id:`html-106`,level:5,topic:`Focus`,type:`single`,prompt:'What does `tabindex="0"` do on a custom element?',choices:[`It adds the element to the normal Tab order`,`It hides the element from screen readers`,`It removes the element from the Tab order`,`It makes the element the first to receive focus`],answer:0,hint:`Zero means "no special position".`,explain:'`tabindex="0"` makes an element focusable in its natural place in the Tab order. Positive values force a custom order, which usually confuses users.'},{id:`html-115`,level:5,topic:`Focus`,type:`multi`,prompt:`Which elements can receive focus with the Tab key by default? Select all that apply.`,choices:['`<a href="/help">`',"`<button>`","`<p>`","`<div>`"],answer:[0,1],hint:`Which ones can the user interact with?`,explain:"Links with `href` and buttons are focusable by default. Plain `<div>` and `<p>` elements are not, because they are not interactive."},{id:`html-118`,level:5,topic:`Video`,type:`fill`,prompt:"Type the attribute name that shows play and pause controls on a `<video>`.",answer:[`controls`],hint:`It is a boolean attribute named after what it shows.`,explain:"Without `controls`, a video has no built-in play button. Add it so every user can start, pause and seek."},{id:`html-100`,level:5,topic:`Audio`,type:`single`,prompt:`Which tag fills both blanks to play this sound file?`,code:`<____ src="song.mp3" controls></____>`,lang:`html`,choices:["`music`","`mp3`","`audio`","`sound`"],answer:2,hint:"It uses the same `src` and `controls` attributes as `<video>`.",explain:"`<audio>` plays sound. The `controls` attribute lets users play, pause and change the volume."},{id:`html-110`,level:5,topic:`Disclosure`,type:`single`,prompt:`Which tag fills both blanks to make a widget the user can open and close without JavaScript?`,code:`<____>
  <summary>What is HTML?</summary>
  <p>The language of web pages.</p>
</____>`,lang:`html`,choices:["`aside`","`details`","`label`","`section`"],answer:1,hint:`It is often used for FAQ answers.`,explain:"`<details>` shows its `<summary>` line and hides the rest until the user opens it. The browser handles the keyboard and state for you."},{id:`html-113`,level:5,topic:`Video`,type:`tf`,prompt:"You can put fallback content between `<video>` and `</video>` for browsers that cannot play it.",answer:!0,hint:`What does a browser do with content it cannot use?`,explain:"Content inside `<video>`, after any `<source>` and `<track>` elements, is shown only when video is not supported. A download link is a common fallback."},{id:`html-109`,level:5,topic:`Attributes`,type:`single`,prompt:"What are `data-*` attributes, like `data-user-id`, for?",code:`<li data-user-id="42">Sam</li>`,lang:`html`,choices:[`Marking content as private so users cannot see it`,`Storing custom values for scripts and CSS to read`,`Sending their values to the server on every page load`,`Connecting an element to a row in a database`],answer:1,hint:`Think about where a script could keep a small value, like a user ID.`,explain:"`data-*` attributes store custom values on elements. JavaScript reads them with `element.dataset`, and CSS can select them."},{id:`html-111`,level:5,topic:`Forms`,type:`single`,prompt:'What does `autocomplete="email"` help with?',choices:[`It lets the browser offer the user's saved email`,`It submits the form as soon as the email is typed`,`It checks with the mail server that the address exists`,`It fills the field with a random test address`],answer:0,hint:`Think of the suggestions your browser shows when you type.`,explain:"`autocomplete` tells the browser what kind of data a field wants, so it can offer saved values. This saves time and helps users with motor or memory difficulties."},{id:`html-108`,level:5,topic:`Scripts`,type:`single`,prompt:"What does `defer` do on this script tag?",code:`<head>
  <script src="app.js" defer><\/script>
</head>`,lang:`html`,choices:[`It waits to run the script until the user clicks the page`,`It runs the script right away, before the page is parsed`,`It stops the script from ever running`,`It runs the script after the whole HTML is parsed`],answer:3,hint:`The word means "put off until later".`,explain:"A deferred script downloads in parallel and runs after the HTML is parsed, in order. So it can safely use page elements, even from `<head>`."},{id:`html-117`,level:5,topic:`Accessible Names`,type:`multi`,prompt:"Which of these give an `<input>` an accessible name? Select all that apply.",choices:["An `aria-label` attribute","A `class` attribute","A `<label>` whose `for` matches the input's `id`","A `name` attribute"],answer:[0,2],hint:`Which ones exist to describe the field to people?`,explain:"A connected `<label>` and `aria-label` both name the field for assistive tech. `name` is the form data key, and `class` is for styling."},{id:`html-120`,level:5,topic:`Attributes`,type:`fill`,prompt:`Type the global attribute name that hides an element from the page.`,answer:[`hidden`],hint:`It is a boolean attribute: you write only its name, with no value.`,explain:"The `hidden` attribute hides an element from display and from assistive tech. CSS can override it, so check your styles if it seems not to work."}]};export{e as default};